\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The real Gaussian law is a product over orthogonal sectors}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{Let #{H} be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space,
#{P:H\to H} an orthogonal involution, and #{Q:H\to H} a
positive self-adjoint precision with #{QP=PQ}. Put
#{H_\varepsilon=\ker(P-\varepsilon I)} and
#{E_\varepsilon=(I+\varepsilon P)/2}. Use
[induced Euclidean volumes](fdip-002M) on the uncompleted Borel
sigma-algebras. The restrictions #{Q_\varepsilon=Q|_{H_\varepsilon}}
are positive self-adjoint. For their [Gaussian laws](fdip-0005),
##{\begin{aligned}
D:H&\longrightarrow H_+\times H_-,\\
z&\longmapsto(E_+z,E_-z),\\
D_*\mu_Q&=\mu_{Q_+}\otimes\mu_{Q_-},\qquad
Z_Q=Z_{Q_+}Z_{Q_-}.
\end{aligned}}
Thus the sector-valued random variables #{E_+,E_-} are independent
with the indicated marginal laws. The
[four-cycle recovery](fdip-003S) computes both blocks, their
normalizers and their retained scalar laws explicitly.}

\proof{
\p{The [module projector identities](fdip-003O) give the direct sum.
For #{u\in H_+}, #{v\in H_-}, orthogonality of #{P} gives
#{\langle u,v\rangle=\langle Pu,Pv\rangle=-\langle u,v\rangle},
so #{\langle u,v\rangle=0}. Addition
#{J:H_+\times H_-\to H}, #{J(u,v)=u+v}, is therefore an
isometric linear isomorphism with inverse #{D}. Commutation gives
#{PQu=QPu=\varepsilon Qu} for #{u\in H_\varepsilon}; hence
#{Q} preserves each sector. Restricting its pairing identity and
testing nonzero sector vectors proves self-adjointness and positivity
of #{Q_\varepsilon}.}

\p{Concatenating orthonormal bases of #{H_+,H_-} gives an
orthonormal basis of #{H}. Coordinate product Lebesgue measure,
transported by these charts, therefore proves
##{J_*(\lambda_+\otimes\lambda_-)=\lambda_H,\qquad
D_*\lambda_H=\lambda_+\otimes\lambda_-.}
This is precisely [orthogonal product volume](fdip-002Q).
Finite-dimensional spaces have countable bases, so the product Borel
sigma-algebra is the Borel sigma-algebra of the product. Bounded
coordinate cubes exhaust each space, proving sigma-finiteness.
A zero-dimensional sector has unit mass on its sole point.}

\p{Orthogonality and preservation of sectors give the action identity
##{\langle u+v,Q(u+v)\rangle
 =\langle u,Q_+u\rangle+\langle v,Q_-v\rangle.}
For #{w_Q(z)=e^{-\langle z,Qz\rangle/2}}, this says
#{w_Q(J(u,v))=w_{Q_+}(u)w_{Q_-}(v)}. The
[positive normalizer theorem](fdip-002T) applies separately to all
three positive self-adjoint maps:
##{Z_R=\frac{(2\pi)^{\dim U/2}}{\sqrt{\det R}}\in(0,\infty)
\quad(R=Q,Q_+,Q_-\text{ on its space }U).}
Its proof uses positive eigenvalues, the scalar Gaussian integral
and nonnegative Tonelli, without presupposing this product law.
Empty determinants and normalizers are one. Applying Tonelli with
the volume and weight identities just proved gives #{Z_Q=Z_{Q_+}Z_{Q_-}}.}

\p{For Borel sets #{A_+\subseteq H_+}, #{A_-\subseteq H_-},
all the following integrands are nonnegative measurable functions:
##{\begin{aligned}
(D_*\mu_Q)(A_+\times A_-)
 &=\frac{1}{Z_{Q_+}Z_{Q_-}}\\
 &\quad\cdot\int_{A_+\times A_-}w_{Q_+}(u)w_{Q_-}(v)
    \,d(\lambda_+\otimes\lambda_-)(u,v)\\
 &=\mu_{Q_+}(A_+)\mu_{Q_-}(A_-).
\end{aligned}}
Rectangles form a pi-system containing the whole product and generating
its Borel sigma-algebra. The pi-lambda uniqueness theorem for finite
measures identifies the two probability measures everywhere.
Their coordinate marginals and the displayed rectangle equation are
exactly independence of #{E_+,E_-}. The argument also covers a
zero sector, whose law is deterministic. Algebraic splitting alone
would not have supplied this probability identity.}
}

\p{For the actual [polynomial function algebras](fdip-002V), write
#{\omega_R(p)=\int p\,d\mu_R}. Then
##{\omega_Q\bigl((p_+\circ E_+)(p_-\circ E_-)\bigr)
 =\omega_{Q_+}(p_+)\omega_{Q_-}(p_-).}
Indeed linear precomposition takes polynomial generators to polynomial
generators. Every polynomial, product and square has finite absolute
Gaussian integral by \ref{fdip-002W}, applied on the full space and
on each sector. Tonelli applied first to the absolute product therefore
justifies signed Fubini, which gives the formula from the product law.
These are [real polynomial states](fdip-002X): they are linear,
normalized and nonnegative on #{p^2}, with the identity star. No
algebra structure on all integrable functions, or state on
#{\operatorname{End}(H)}, is being used.}

\p{\citet{Proposition B.55, p. 505}{friedli2017statistical}
uses covariance as its positive parameter. In an orthonormal chart,
set #{\Sigma=Q^{-1}}. Its eigenvalues are positive,
#{\Sigma^{-1}=Q} and #{\det\Sigma=(\det Q)^{-1}}, so its
normalized density is exactly #{Z_Q^{-1}w_Q}. The
[inverse-precision covariance theorem](fdip-000B) identifies
#{\Sigma} as the covariance. The product proof above uses densities,
not an inference from zero covariance for an unspecified law.}
\p{Mathlib's \leanref{OrthonormalBasis.measurePreserving_repr}
supports passage to orthonormal coordinates. Index coordinates by the
disjoint union of orthonormal bases of #{H_+} and #{H_-}. Then
#{\Sigma=\operatorname{diag}(Q_+^{-1},Q_-^{-1})} is positive
definite, hence positive semidefinite, with zero cross block and mean
zero. After the density identification, TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.indepFun_sumEquivProd_multivariateGaussian_iff}
gives the corresponding coordinate independence. It concerns this
Gaussian covariance, not an arbitrary algebraic splitting or Schur
fiber integral.}
\p{The integration convention is
\citet{Theorem 1.7.15, pp. 200-201, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.
All probability assertions here are finite-dimensional and real, with
strictly positive precision and specified volume. They supply no
Lebesgue probability over a general coefficient ring; a singular
precision needs a separately specified constrained space and law.}
